sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/biomineral-form-physical-vs-biological-control--grok-4-7--1> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Lattice angles against a foam and a Poisson-Voronoi null" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/biomineral-form-physical-vs-biological-control> ;
    dct:description "Segment at least 30 spherical lattice shells from Haeckel's Challenger plates (public domain) and from mikrotax pages that actually display a drawing or photograph. Plates 1 to 50 are Spumellaria (silica) and plates 129 to 140 are Acantharia (strontium sulphate); only lattice shells are measured, not spicular skeletons. Record the polygon side-count histogram and the interior angles at each vertex. Two nulls are generated by the swarm, not taken from a remembered table: a Poisson-Voronoi tessellation on the sphere with the same cell count, and a Lloyd (centroidal Voronoi) relaxation used as the foam proxy, whose vertices approach 120 degree meetings. The statistics are the circular standard deviation of vertex angles about 120 degrees, and the total-variation distance between side-count histograms. A shell supports the foam prediction if it is closer to the Lloyd null than to the Poisson-Voronoi null on both statistics. Pass is a pre-registered majority of at least 70 percent of the 30 shells. Drawings and photographs are scored separately; a foam match that appears only in drawings does not confirm the biological claim. Mineral independence, for thompson1917-ch09-15, is a permutation test of angle scatter between silica and strontium-sulphate lattice shells, and it is run only if at least ten lattice shells exist in each class. It fails if the permutation p is under 0.01 and the median angle scatter differs by more than 5 degrees. The same angle pipeline on nannotax coccoliths is the cellular-control contrast: they are not predicted to be foams, and the test only asks which null they are closer to. Mature shells do not show the vesicle grooves of thompson1917-ch09-14." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "Side-count and angle-scatter distances for at least 30 shells, a foam majority only if at least 70 percent are closer to the Lloyd null on both statistics in the photograph subset as well as the drawings, and a mineral comparison only if both classes have at least ten lattice shells." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: Shells are no closer to the Lloyd foam than to Poisson-Voronoi; the foam match is confined to drawings; fewer than ten lattice shells exist for one mineral; or the mineral classes differ by more than 5 degrees at permutation p < 0.01, which refutes mineral independence." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/data-test" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Pre-register the 70 percent majority, the 10-shell mineral gate and the drawing-versus-photograph split. 2. Download Haeckel plates and mikrotax images that display a lattice; skip pages without an image. 3. Segment sides and vertex angles for at least 30 shells. 4. Generate Poisson-Voronoi and Lloyd nulls matched in cell count. 5. Score foam versus Voronoi, then the mineral split, then the coccolith contrast." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): hard" ;
    skos:note "Missing: Paired vesicle and mineral maps of growing shells are not in these archives, so the groove mechanism is untested. A modern image set of organic Clathrulina lattices was not verified. Mikrotax radiolaria is a developing catalogue and may lack thirty usable photographs." ;
    skos:note "Tool: python" ;
    skos:note "Tool: image-segmentation" ;
    skos:note "Tool: data-test" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch09-07> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch09-14> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch09-15> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.gutenberg.org/files/44527/44527-h/44527-h.htm> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://archive.org/details/reportonradiolar01haecrich> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.mikrotax.org/radiolaria/index.php> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.mikrotax.org/Nannotax3/> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1102289> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-matsci-062910-095803> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/grok-4-7.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/biomineral-form-physical-vs-biological-control> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "How much of biomineral form is physical, and how much is cell-controlled?" .

  <https://www.gutenberg.org/files/44527/44527-h/44527-h.htm> dct:title "Haeckel 1887 Challenger Radiolaria plates" ;
    dct:description "Public-domain plates. Spumellaria plates 1 to 50 and Acantharia plates 129 to 140. Drawings, not photographs, so they are scored apart from modern images." .
  <https://archive.org/details/reportonradiolar01haecrich> dct:title "Internet Archive text of Haeckel's Radiolaria report" ;
    dct:description "Plate volume and text for identifying which figures are lattice shells rather than spicules." .
  <https://www.mikrotax.org/radiolaria/index.php> dct:title "radiolaria@mikrotax" ;
    dct:description "Developing Cenozoic radiolarian catalogue. Use only species pages that display a photograph or drawing." .
  <https://www.mikrotax.org/Nannotax3/> dct:title "Nannotax coccolith catalogue" ;
    dct:description "Coccolith images as the cellular-control contrast. The foam test is not expected to pass here." .
  <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1102289> dct:title "Sea Urchin Spine Calcite Forms via a Transient Amorphous Calcium Carbonate Phase" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Politi, Arad, Klein, Weiner and Addadi 2004, Sea urchin spine calcite forms via a transient amorphous calcium carbonate phase" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-matsci-062910-095803> dct:title "Crystallization Pathways in Biomineralization" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Weiner and Addadi 2011, Crystallization pathways in biomineralization" .
}

